//! iridium_loop — a bounded build/review loop over the iridium editor library.
//!
//! Shape, identical to `mm_fleet_loop.awl`'s: an OVERSIGHT seat (an ACP Claude
//! session) plans the pass and judges it; a BUILD seat (a norn Sol session)
//! executes it; the SERVER runs the gate battery itself as a declared command,
//! so the exit codes are the machine's and not an agent's account of them. The
//! judgment seat answers exactly CONTINUE or DONE, and CONTINUE routes
//! backward to the plan step — a bounded flow loop over the operator's
//! `max_passes`.
//!
//! ⭐ Authored FOR Robo Doug, by Vesper Lynd, 2026-08-12. The SHAPE is copied
//! from the market-mirror document; the CONTENT deliberately is not. The
//! briefs there carry market-mirror's own contract (no real money, no wallet
//! keys, no order placement) which is meaningless here and would have taught
//! this project's agents rules belonging to another one. Harness values and
//! project prose do not travel between documents — only the machinery does.
//!
//! 🔴 THE ANCHOR STRING MUST BE CHANGED BEFORE THE FIRST RUN. See the comment
//! on `control_expect` below; this is not cosmetic and it is not optional.
workflow iridium_loop
  input repo: String
  input task: String
  input max_passes: Int
  outcome done:         type LoopReport, route success
  outcome exhausted:    type LoopReport, route failure
  outcome off_protocol: type Refused,    route failure
  outcome unmeasured:   type Refused,    route failure

/// 🔴 CHANGE THIS, AND CHANGE THE FIRST LINE OF
/// `docs/CONTROL-ANCHOR.txt` TO MATCH — the file currently carries
/// market-mirror's string verbatim, and an IDENTICAL anchor in two projects
/// DISARMS THE CONTROL IT IS PART OF.
///
/// The control asks the seams to read the anchor RELATIVE to their own working
/// directory, precisely so that an agent standing in the wrong tree is caught.
/// But if both trees answer with the same string, an agent that lands in
/// market-mirror instead of iridium reads a DIFFERENT FILE, returns the SAME
/// LINE, and passes. Right and plausible-wrong coincide, and the guard
/// distinguishes nothing.
///
/// A distinct string per project is the whole fix. It is not a secret and does
/// not need to be: what it establishes is which tree you are standing in, not
/// who you are.
/// ⚠️ MUST be unique to THIS repository, not merely to this workflow. The
/// anchor is read relatively, so an agent in the wrong tree opens a different
/// file — but that only catches anything if the two files ANSWER DIFFERENTLY.
/// This document's first draft carried market-mirror's string verbatim, and
/// with the same line in both trees a misplaced agent would have read the
/// wrong file, returned the right string, and passed. (Vesper Lynd, 12 Aug
/// 2026, before any run.)
const control_expect = "iridium-control-anchor-dcf26d-2026-08-12"

const control_brief = """
You are a control leg of a fleet workflow. Read the file at the path given
below, RELATIVE TO YOUR OWN WORKING DIRECTORY — do not resolve it against
anything else. Reply with EXACTLY its first line and nothing else: no
greeting, no punctuation of your own, no commentary.

File: """

/// RELATIVE on purpose. An ABSOLUTE path proves the seam is alive, obedient
/// and can read that one file — and passes identically whether the agent is
/// standing in this repository or another one, because an absolute path
/// resolves the same from anywhere. Relative makes the working directory
/// load-bearing, so one question tests both facts: the seam can read the tree
/// AND it is standing in it. (Robo Doug's own finding, 2026-08-12.)
const control_anchor_path = "docs/CONTROL-ANCHOR.txt"

const ctrl_sep = " /// "

const plan_brief = """
You are the COORDINATOR of a fleet workflow: the planning seat. Work
read-only in this step — change no files, commit nothing.

Read, inside the repository root given below: the workspace manifest, every
crate under the workspace, the tests, and any docs/ present. iridium is a
Rust editor library: `iridium-editor` (the kernel, which must build with
--no-default-features), `iridium-lang`, and `iridium-bindings` (which must
also build for wasm32-unknown-unknown under --features web).

Write THE PLAN FOR THE NEXT PASS ONLY. Rules, all binding:
- The smallest pass that makes real progress against the task below.
- Name the concrete gap it closes, the files it touches, and which targeted
  tests prove it.
- Feature-gating is load-bearing here: the kernel builds WITHOUT default
  features and the bindings build for wasm. A change that only compiles under
  --all-features is not done.
- Prefer a plan the gate battery can actually settle. `scripts/ci.sh` runs
  four test legs, two check/clippy wasm legs, three clippy legs and fmt.

Task, and then the record of earlier passes: """

const build_brief = """
You are the BUILDER of a fleet workflow. Execute the coordinator's plan
below, and only it. Rules, all binding:

- Touch only the files the plan names. Never run repository-wide formatters
  or fixers beyond `cargo fmt` on what you changed.
- The kernel must keep compiling with --no-default-features, and
  `iridium-bindings` must keep compiling for wasm32-unknown-unknown under
  --features web. If your change breaks either, it is not finished.
- Clippy runs with `-D warnings` across four configurations. `#[allow]`,
  `#[expect]` and `_var` renames are bypasses, not fixes — fix the code.
- Prove YOUR change with its targeted tests (for example
  `cargo test -p iridium-lang`). The full battery runs mechanically after you
  finish, so do not run it and do not claim it.
- NEVER commit, branch, tag or push. Landing is the operator's hand. Leave
  the work as clean modified files.

⛔ BANNED COMMANDS. Each is named individually and none is a category you may
reason your way around. Do not run, in this checkout, for any reason:

    git stash          git checkout       git checkout --
    git restore        git reset          git clean
    git worktree       git add -A         git rebase

Every one of them can destroy work that is not yours and that you cannot see.
There is exactly one sanctioned way to put a file back as it was:

    git show HEAD:<path> > <path>

`git mv` is permitted. If you believe you need one of the banned commands,
STOP and say so in your report instead — being blocked and saying why is a
correct outcome, and is infinitely better than being unblocked and wrong.

⚠️ This is a LIVE checkout that a person is working in. Files you did not
touch may be modified for reasons that have nothing to do with you. Stage
nothing, revert nothing you did not write, and never operate on "all changes".

Report back: what you changed (every file, with why), the exact commands you
ran with their outcomes, and anything you could NOT do with the honest
reason. Do not claim a gate you did not run.

Repository root: """

const review_brief = """
You are the REVIEWER of a fleet workflow: a rigorous reviewer from a
different model family than the builder, not a cheerleader. Review the
CURRENT uncommitted work (git diff and new files) in the repository root
below, against the plan and against the standard this codebase holds:
complete implementations, no silent failures, no swallowed Results, no
arbitrary limits or assumed defaults, errors handled or propagated.

Judge what the diff DOES, not what the builder's report SAYS it does. The
gate battery's own verdict token travels with this brief; if the builder's
account and the battery disagree, the battery is the fact.

Name every real defect. There is no such thing as a minor one.

Repository root: """

const decide_brief = """
You are the JUDGMENT seat. Given the task, the gate battery's verdict token
and the reviewer's verdict below, answer with EXACTLY ONE WORD:

  CONTINUE  — more passes are needed
  DONE      — the task is complete and the battery is green

No punctuation, no explanation, no other word. Anything else refuses the run
as off-protocol, which is correct: a judgment seat that will not answer the
question asked has not judged.

Task: """

const task_bridge = """

TASK:
"""

const history_bridge = """

RECORD OF EARLIER PASSES (empty on the first pass):
"""

const plan_bridge = """

THE PLAN:
"""

const build_report_bridge = """

THE BUILDER'S OWN REPORT:
"""

const gate_tail_bridge = """

GATE BATTERY LOG (stderr; the token is separate and authoritative):
"""

const gate_token_bridge = """

GATE BATTERY VERDICT TOKEN:
"""

const review_verdict_bridge = """

THE REVIEWER'S VERDICT:
"""

const pass_open = """

=== PASS ===
PLAN:
"""

const pass_review = """
REVIEW:
"""

const pass_gates = """
GATE TOKEN:
"""

const pass_decision = """
DECISION:
"""

/// The canonical agent-outcome record every dispatch of an agent seam
/// returns: the leg's terminal `text` and the canonical `stop_reason`.
type Reply { text: String, stop_reason: String }

/// What the server records for a declared command: the real exit status and
/// both streams. Under `--verdict` the exit status says whether the battery
/// RAN; the token on stdout says whether it PASSED. Never read one for the
/// other.
type GateLeg { exit_code: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String }

/// The state threaded through the pass loop.
type CycleState { history: String, decision: String, review: String, build_report: String }

/// The terminal account of a finished run.
type LoopReport { passes: Int, decision: String, last_review: String, history: String }

/// A run that refuses rather than guesses, said out loud.
type Refused { reason: String, detail: String }

worker iridium_build
  /// The BUILD seam. Serve with:
  /// aion worker agent workflows/iridium_loop.awl
  ///   --task-queue iridium_build --identity <name> --liminal-address <h:p>
  /// ⚠️ The queue name is deliberately NOT `mm_loop_build`. That queue has
  /// live workers on this box serving another project's document; a worker
  /// joining it would take that project's dispatches.
  harness
    kind norn
    /// Robo Doug's value: this loop is strictly sequential, one build per
    /// pass, so a second slot is capacity the shape can never use.
    concurrency 1
    reconnect_initial_backoff 5s
    reconnect_max_backoff 30s
    reconnect_max_attempts 6
    /// The agent subprocess's WHOLE environment: what is not named here is
    /// ABSENT in the child whatever this worker inherits.
    /// ⚠️ Robo Doug's list omits USER and LOGNAME. On the market-mirror
    /// document those two are present because a scrubbed-environment agent
    /// could not find its login without them and answered "Not logged in"
    /// instead of failing loudly. Measured there, not assumed — if this seam
    /// reports itself logged out, this line is the first place to look.
    env_pass "PATH", "HOME", "TMPDIR", "USER", "LOGNAME"
    /// `binary` deliberately omitted: norn resolves on PATH here.
    args "--account", "default"
  action build(prompt: String) -> Reply
    agent, timeout 90m, retry 3 backoff 30s..5m

worker iridium_oversight
  /// The OVERSIGHT seam — plan, review, judgment. Serve with:
  /// aion worker agent workflows/iridium_loop.awl
  ///   --task-queue iridium_oversight --identity <name> --liminal-address <h:p>
  harness
    kind acp
    concurrency 1
    reconnect_initial_backoff 5s
    reconnect_max_backoff 30s
    reconnect_max_attempts 6
    env_pass "PATH", "HOME", "TMPDIR", "USER", "LOGNAME"
    /// Resolved through fnm's `default` alias rather than the pinned
    /// node-version directory: measured 2026-08-12, both resolve to the same
    /// file, but the version-pinned one dies silently on a node upgrade while
    /// this one follows `fnm default`. Not unconditionally stable — it still
    /// breaks if a node is installed WITHOUT being made the default. There is
    /// no stable path here, only a later-breaking one.
    /// The language REQUIRES an absolute path for this setting and refuses a
    /// bare name: a worker starts from whatever directory a supervisor or a
    /// shell happened to be in, so a short name can resolve differently on
    /// every launch.
    command "/Users/tom/.local/share/fnm/aliases/default/bin/claude-code-acp"
    cwd "/Users/tom/Developer/ablative/libs/iridium"
    /// 🔴 Robo Doug's ruling for run 1: READ-ONLY. This seam plans, reviews
    /// and judges; with `deny` it cannot write files or run commands, so a
    /// first run demonstrates the machinery without letting an unattended
    /// agent act. The BUILD seam above is the one that changes the tree.
    /// There is no default for this setting by design — it is exactly the
    /// decision nobody should be able to make by omission.
    permission "deny"
    exit_grace 10s
  action oversee(prompt: String) -> Reply
    agent, timeout 90m, retry 3 backoff 30s..5m

worker iridium_gates
  /// The gate battery, run BY THE SERVER as a declared command sealed into
  /// the package identity at deploy — not by an agent, and not reported by
  /// one. `--verdict` exits 0 whenever the battery RAN; the token on stdout
  /// (`pass` / `fail`) is the verdict, and an unmeasured run emits NO TOKEN
  /// AT ALL rather than the plausible lie `fail`.
  /// ⚠️ NO RETRY CLAUSE, deliberately: a red battery is a result to work on,
  /// not an error to absorb. Retrying it would hide the very thing the loop
  /// exists to fix.
  action gates(repo: String) -> GateLeg
    run "zsh $repo/scripts/ci.sh --verdict"

/// Ask a question whose answer is already known, before spending hours on
/// ones that matter. A broken pipeline and a working one both return
/// well-formed prose; only a known answer tells them apart. Both seams are
/// asked, so it also proves they are pointed at the SAME tree.
step control
  control_brief + control_anchor_path -> control_prompt
  build(prompt: control_prompt) -> build_ctrl
  oversee(prompt: control_prompt) -> oversee_ctrl
  build_ctrl.text + ctrl_sep + oversee_ctrl.text -> ctrl_pair
  outcome proven: when build_ctrl.text == control_expect and oversee_ctrl.text == control_expect,
    route passes
  outcome broken: otherwise,
    route unmeasured(
      reason: "a control delegation did not return the anchor line, so this pipeline cannot be shown to read the tree or follow an exact instruction — no work was attempted (build reply, then oversight reply, in detail)",
      detail: ctrl_pair)

/// The pass loop: plan → build → gate → review → decide, threading the pass
/// record. Bounded by the operator's max_passes; the body runs at least once.
step passes
  loop state = CycleState(history: "", decision: "CONTINUE", review: "", build_report: "") counting pass_count
    plan_brief + repo + task_bridge + task + history_bridge + state.history -> plan_prompt
    oversee(prompt: plan_prompt) -> plan
    build_brief + repo + task_bridge + task + plan_bridge + plan.text -> build_prompt
    build(prompt: build_prompt) -> built
    gates(repo: repo) -> gate_leg
    review_brief + repo + task_bridge + task + plan_bridge + plan.text + build_report_bridge + built.text + gate_token_bridge + gate_leg.stdout + gate_tail_bridge + gate_leg.stderr -> review_prompt
    oversee(prompt: review_prompt) -> reviewed
    decide_brief + task + gate_token_bridge + gate_leg.stdout + review_verdict_bridge + reviewed.text -> decide_prompt
    oversee(prompt: decide_prompt) -> decision
    state.history + pass_open + plan.text + pass_review + reviewed.text + pass_gates + gate_leg.stdout + pass_decision + decision.text -> new_history
    CycleState(history: new_history, decision: decision.text, review: reviewed.text, build_report: built.text) -> state
    until not state.decision == "CONTINUE"
    max max_passes
  on failure
    route unmeasured(reason: "a leg of the pass could not run to completion — an agent seam died past its retries, or the gate battery could not MEASURE (ci.sh refused with exit 2 or 3, or the leg died outright). No pass outcome exists, so none is reported.", detail: "see the run's history and transcripts for the failed activity")
  outcome finished: when state.decision == "DONE",
    route done(passes: pass_count, decision: state.decision, last_review: state.review,
      history: state.history)
  outcome spent: when state.decision == "CONTINUE",
    route exhausted(passes: pass_count, decision: state.decision, last_review: state.review,
      history: state.history)
  outcome off: otherwise,
    route off_protocol(
      reason: "the judgment seat's decision reply was not exactly CONTINUE or DONE — an off-protocol coordinator is a refusal, never a guess (the reply is in detail)",
      detail: state.decision)
